  "_notExpressed": [
    "1. A state a pair STARTS in. Every first meeting is `fresh`, but in a fourth season most pairs have a past: a `pairs[].startState` (used while meetings is 0) would let a marquee pair open `simmering` or `settled`.",
    "2. Default types by circle. defaultPair is flat; twelve fighters need a `circles` table (fighter to circle) and a `circlePairs` table (two circles to types), so 66 pairs need no row each.",
    "3. A tone a state adds. states have no `tones`, so a simmering plot cannot lean grave except through weights. A `states.<id>.tones` filter, intersected with the type's, would do it.",
    "4. A gesture that depends on role AND arrival order together (the teacher waits calmly, the student waits nervously). `wait` rows can name only first, second, left or right, so no `wait` gesture is written.",
    "5. A per-fighter gesture veto or swap (harden, or defer when his voice vetoes it). fighters has neverStance and neverType but no neverGesture.",
    "6. A stance or an angle that changes by slot (wry at the arrival, cold at the stare). Both are one per fighter for the whole intro.",
    "7. A tie rule for two rows naming the same fighter at the same point. The rows here avoid it by convention (state gestures at look_start and land_first, type gestures at land_second and look_end); the resolver should drop the type's row if they meet.",
    "8. Event weights. pairs[].events are an ordered list, with no weight between ledger events.",
    "9. A pair's own register (the Protagonist warm to the rival, the rival contemptuous). Stance lists are per type and role, so the pair's register comes only from the fighters' vetoes: here the Protagonist never says formal, cold or smug, so a rivalry leader falls through to warm for him and formal for the rival, but a chaser stays guarded for both. A `fighters.<id>.stanceTo.<other>` would say it directly.",
    "10. A `look` of `away` (one breaks the stare first): there is no such part or pose yet."
  ]
